course: dictionary
lesson: 14971
title: Remunerate means pay for work
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To remunerate someone means to pay them for their work or a service. It is a formal word, so you will meet it in letters, contracts and job adverts, where adults talk about money. «The company will remunerate every worker for extra hours.» Here, remunerate tells you that the workers will be paid for their extra time.
explain: Children would usually say pay. «The club will pay the coach for his time» means the same as «The club will remunerate the coach for his time.» Try swapping pay into the sentence: if it still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: Look for the clues around the word. «The gardener was well remunerated for cutting the lawns all summer.» The word well tells you the payment was good, and the words for cutting the lawns tell you what the work was for.
fact: Remunerate is a verb: you remunerate, he remunerates, they remunerated, they are remunerating.
fact: Remunerate means to pay someone for work or a service.
fact: Remunerate is formal, so adults use it in contracts and job adverts.
fact: «Well remunerated» means paid a good amount for the work.
fact: Children can say pay instead: «The shop will pay you for the job» means the same thing.
exercise: «The company will remunerate every worker for extra hours.» What does remunerate mean? | To work for longer | To give someone a holiday | *To pay someone for work | To thank someone with a card
exercise: Which everyday word could replace remunerate? | Borrow | Hide | Forget | *Pay
exercise: «The gardener was well remunerated for cutting the lawns.» What does well remunerated mean? | Given a warm welcome | Tired out | *Paid a good amount for the job | Cut very neatly
test: «The club will remunerate the coach for his time.» Who gets the money in this sentence? | The time | *The coach | The club | The lawns
test: Which sentence is about being paid for work? | The sky was remunerated with blue. | *The nurses were remunerated for their night shifts. | The river remunerated the bridge.
exam: What is the best everyday word to use instead of remunerate? | *Pay | Hide | Smile | Forget
